WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.450 --> 00:00:06.620
It was a little past

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:11.550
midnight when I got the call asking me
to knock doors for Hillary Clinton.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.650
The caller was my friend Jason, who was
working on the campaign in Pennsylvania.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:23.630
He wanted me to take time off of my
job to help with the final push.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.800
Why me, I asked him?

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.400
Isn't there someone else more qualified?

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.740
At the time, I was serving as
a policy advisor at the White House.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.495
And what I was actually wondering was,

00:00:39.495 --> 00:00:42.716
isn't there someone else
less overqualified?

00:00:42.716 --> 00:00:47.960
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I was working on change at scale.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.440
If I got this policy done
on self-driving cars,

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.210
we would potentially be saving
over 40,000 lives per year.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.559
And if I took time off of work,
I would knock how many doors?

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.400
I also didn't know the first thing about
talking to a stranger at their front door.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.560
What I knew about was policy.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:16.220
The decision seemed obvious,
I could add more value right where I was.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:18.750
Sorry, I said.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:21.680
But Jason didn't give up.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.617
You want to make a difference,
come knock doors.

00:01:28.598 --> 00:01:33.627
I didn't believe him, but I went anyways,
because a friend had asked.

00:01:33.627 --> 00:01:38.104
And I am so glad that I did.

00:01:38.104 --> 00:01:43.082
Because for a long time I thought that
the way to create change was top down, and

00:01:43.082 --> 00:01:46.554
that meant advising government
leaders at McKinsey or

00:01:46.554 --> 00:01:51.480
creating policy at the White House,
not knocking on doors in Pennsylvania.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:58.566
Maybe some of you have a similar view, But

00:01:58.566 --> 00:02:02.650
I want to talk to you about why
bottom up change matters too.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:10.030
Knocking on doors can change elections,
change you, and change your world today.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:15.410
So what does it mean to knock on doors?

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:18.240
Knocking on doors is about
getting into a neighborhood and

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.050
going door to door to talk to voters.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:25.770
It has two primary purposes,
persuading voters and increasing turnout.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:32.010
Knocking on doors might feel old school,
but it makes sense.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.660
Think about how you change someone's mind.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:38.980
It's face to face through
a relationship built on trust.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:42.799
Knocking on doors relies on both of
those strategies for persuading voters.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:47.210
It's also an effective tool for
driving turnout.

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:50.170
Talking to voters can provide
the nudge that they need

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.730
to actually get out there and
vote on election day.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:03:00.009
I remember the first time I knocked
on doors I thought, this works.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:05.340
I remember the woman in Pittsburg
who told me she wasn't going to

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.810
vote because she didn't know
where her polling location was.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:11.370
Just the kind of
information I had on hand.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:17.370
At another home, a man with a golden
retriever stood at the door and

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:19.740
said he wasn't going to vote
because Hillary had it in the bag.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:24.585
I showed them the latest polling
numbers and he promised me he'd vote.

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:29.547
After that experience,
I dug into the research and

00:03:29.547 --> 00:03:34.204
found out that two decades of studies
have shown that the surest way to change

00:03:34.204 --> 00:03:37.860
an election outcome is to have
high quality door knocking.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.005
Talking to voters and getting out
the vote can make all the difference,

00:03:43.005 --> 00:03:45.480
especially when margins are tight.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:48.760
Take Virginia.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:53.689
In 2017, a heated local race
between Democrat Shelly Simonds and

00:03:53.689 --> 00:03:56.540
Republican David Yancey ended in a tie.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.830
They ended up deciding the winner
by pulling a name out of a hat.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:08.170
That one race resulted in Republicans
maintaining control of the state house.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:12.400
That means one vote could
have changed what healthcare,

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.810
wages, schools looked like for the state.

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:20.400
And not every election is won by one vote,
but

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.020
every election cycle there
are races with razor thin margins.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:29.690
Knocking on doors is also essential for
national victories.

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:37.002
In 2016, 120,000,000 votes were cast for
the presidential election, but

00:04:37.002 --> 00:04:43.115
just 107,000 of those votes in three
states determined the outcome.

00:04:43.115 --> 00:04:46.343
107,000 votes.

00:04:46.343 --> 00:04:48.085
To put that in perspective,

00:04:48.085 --> 00:04:52.441
if each of us at the business school
had spoken with just 125 voters,

00:04:52.441 --> 00:04:57.030
which by the way you can do in one day,
we would've spoken with all of them.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:02.720
Yes, elections feel big and daunting,
but ultimately they're won by

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:07.690
relatively small numbers, and
those small numbers have huge impact.

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:12.590
Knocking on doors can also change you.

00:05:14.268 --> 00:05:18.930
Let's face it, it can be intimidating
to knock on a stranger's door.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:27.150
But we also know that it's in moments
of discomfort that we grow the most.

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:32.170
And you'll grow tremendously,
both as a leader and as a citizen.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.330
Today, our world is not set up to interact
with people who are different from us.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.700
Instead of taking public transportation,
we take Ubers.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.500
Instead of going to the movies,
we opt for Netflix in bed.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:54.950
And when we want a cup of sugar, we don't
go to our neighbors, we go to Instacart.

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:57.470
Knocking doors gets you
out of that bubble.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:02.040
No two conversations I've had at
the door have ever been the same.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:07.620
As a result, I've practiced and
built a range of skills.

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:12.309
I have more empathy after listening
to the stories of strangers.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:19.530
I take more risk after rejection after
rejection at the door, and I'm much more

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:24.630
comfortable in difficult conversations
after navigating some hostile attitudes.

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:31.160
All of those skills translate in
a really real way to the workplace.

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:32.600
If you're an entrepreneur,

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.220
you'll be that much better at
uncovering the needs of your customers.

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:41.170
If you're an investor, you'll be more
persuasive when it comes to raising money.

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:47.770
For me, as a policymaker,
I'm much better at analyzing the effects

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:52.840
of my policies and programs on a range of
citizens outside of my immediate circle.

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:56.160
I'm also a more informed voter.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:02.030
While I might not be personally struggling
with access to healthcare, thank you,

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:07.260
Stanford Cardinal Care,
after a conversation in Virginia Beach

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:12.140
with a woman whose daughter was
struggling with diabetes, I have this

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:17.310
window into how healthcare policy affects
lives and why it matters to go for it.

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:24.850
So knocking on doors doesn't just
change elections, it also changes you.

00:07:27.170 --> 00:07:30.840
Here at the Business School
my peers often say,

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.900
I want to make a difference, but let me
focus on making it to the top first, and

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:41.000
then I'll do a lot with that money,
power, and position to do a lot of good.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:45.400
But we're at a critical moment right now.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:51.197
On climate change, on health care,
on the future of our workforce,

00:07:51.197 --> 00:07:54.067
our democratic processes are crumbling.

00:07:54.067 --> 00:07:57.969
Some things aren't reversible, and so

00:07:57.969 --> 00:08:02.109
we have to start changing our world today.

00:08:02.109 --> 00:08:06.684
When I came to campus back in the fall,
I was excited to be surrounded by

00:08:06.684 --> 00:08:10.730
my peers who were eager to get
out there and make a difference.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.540
When the 2018 midterm
election rolled around,

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:19.610
100 students expressed
interest in getting involved.

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:25.832
But on the day that it came down
to actually knocking doors,

00:08:25.832 --> 00:08:28.543
only two others students showed up.

00:08:31.994 --> 00:08:36.640
The district we were knocking in was Josh
Harder's, in Central Valley, California.

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:44.180
He won the election, but it was so
close it took them a week to call it.

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:46.525
Our door knocking made a difference.

00:08:48.723 --> 00:08:53.540
Very few of us are probably going to
knock on doors as our full-time job.

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:58.190
That's likely not the way that we aspire
to change lives, change organizations, and

00:08:58.190 --> 00:08:58.889
change the world.

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.950
But there isn't a trade-off here,
you can pursue your

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:08.040
career of top down change while
still knocking on doors today.

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:10.920
They aren't mutually exclusive.

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:14.420
In fact, they're mutually beneficial.

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:20.359
We have an election coming up in 2020.

00:09:20.359 --> 00:09:24.604
Pick a race, it could be something local,
it could be for

00:09:24.604 --> 00:09:27.705
the next president of the United States.

00:09:27.705 --> 00:09:32.013
And commit to getting into a neighborhood,
even if just once,

00:09:32.013 --> 00:09:33.735
and knock on some doors.

00:09:33.735 --> 00:09:35.600
I'll see you out there, thank you.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.684
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:37.684 --> 00:09:42.704
[MUSIC]

